.TH SETUP-VLHE 8 "2026-10-05" "VLHE 1.0.1" "VLHE Manual"
.SH NAME
setup-vlhe \- set the machine's VLHE settings from a menu
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B setup-vlhe
.RB [ \-f
.IR FILE ]
.br
.B setup-vlhe
.BR \-h | \-\-help
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B setup-vlhe
writes the machine's VLHE configuration,
.IR /etc/vlhe.conf ,
from a numbered menu on the console. It writes the configuration file
and nothing else: it does not load modules, start daemons or change
the boot sequence. To apply what it wrote, run
.B vlhe apply
or reboot.
.PP
It is normally run as root, directly or as
.BR "vlhe setup" .
It is a plain
.B /bin/sh
script and needs no X server.
.PP
The main menu has eight rows: an Enable row and a Settings submenu for
each of the three parts of VLHE - Enable Sound Module and Sound
Settings, Enable MIDI and Midi Settings, Enable CD and CD Settings -
then the Debugging and Advanced submenus. The Enable rows say whether
that part is loaded at boot. The submenus carry the control centre's
page names, and their settings use the control centre's wording.
.PP
Each row shows its setting's current value. Type a row's number to
change it: a list of the values offered opens, the current one marked
.BR \-\-> ,
and for most settings a line saying what it is for. Type a value's number to
choose it, or press Enter to leave it as it is; either way the menu
comes back at once, showing the new value. A setting that takes a
number or a device name asks for the value to be typed instead. At the
.B Choice:
prompt:
.TP
.I number
Change that setting, or open that submenu.
.TP
.B s
Save: write the file and exit.
.TP
.B q
Quit without writing anything.
.TP
.B b
Back to the previous menu, from a submenu.
.PP
Each value is checked as it is entered and again before the file is
written. If the file cannot be written, the settings stay on screen;
fix the problem and press
.B s
again, or
.B q
to discard them.
.PP
On a terminal that can be cleared the menu redraws in place; on a dumb
terminal, or with no
.BR TERM ,
it scrolls.
.SH SETTINGS
The menus offer these, each written to the key named in brackets.
.BR vlhe.conf (5)
says what each does.
.PP
The main menu:
.PP
.PD 0
.TP 34
Enable Sound Module
[Sound Settings] LoadAtBoot
.TP
Enable MIDI
[Midi Settings] LoadAtBoot
.TP
Enable CD
[CD Settings] LoadAtBoot
.PD
.PP
Sound Settings:
.PP
.PD 0
.TP 34
Play through (the real sound card)
[Sound Settings] CardDevice
.TP
Programs use
[Sound Settings] ProgramsUse
.TP
Reserve a channel for the MIDI synth
[Sound Settings] MidiChannel
.TP
Release the card when the last program stops
[Sound Settings] ReleaseOnIdle
.TP
Mixing rate
[Sound Settings] MixRate
.TP
Limiter
[Sound Settings] Limiter
.TP
Mix level
[Sound Settings] Attenuation
.TP
Save and restore the sound card's mixer levels
[Sound Settings] SaveMixerLevels
.TP
Keep each program's level across restarts
[Sound Settings] SaveProgramLevels
.PD
.PP
Midi Settings:
.PP
.PD 0
.TP 34
General MIDI font
[Midi Settings] DefaultFont0
.TP
Song font (bank 1)
[Midi Settings] DefaultFont1
.TP
Maximum voices
[Midi Settings] Voices
.TP
Lower voices automatically
[Midi Settings] AutoVoices
.TP
Master gain, per cent
[Midi Settings] Gain, written in thousandths
.TP
Volume curve
[Midi Settings] Law
.TP
Velocity filter
[Midi Settings] Filter
.TP
Sample rate
[Midi Settings] Rate
.TP
Reverb
[Midi Settings] Reverb
.TP
Chorus
[Midi Settings] Chorus
.TP
Modulation envelope
[Midi Settings] ModEnv
.PD
.PP
The two font settings open a list of the SoundFonts found in your own
.B [Fonts] Search
folders and in
.IR /usr/share/sounds/sf2 ,
.I /usr/local/share/sounds/sf2
and
.IR ~/.vlhe/sf2 ,
nine to a page: 10 shows the next page, 0 the one before, n clears
the setting and o takes a typed path. The synth runs as the
.B vlhe
account, so a font it cannot read is marked. A song font needs the
General MIDI font set first, and the General MIDI font cannot be
cleared while a song font is set.
.PP
CD Settings:
.PP
.PD 0
.TP 34
Virtual drives
[CD Settings] Drives
.TP
Present drive as (block major)
[CD Settings] Major
.TP
Reattach the last images at boot
[CD Settings] DrivesAutoLoad
.PD
.PP
Debugging:
.PP
.PD 0
.TP 34
Debug tracing
[Tracing] Enabled
.TP
Save the traces to a run folder
[Tracing] Capture
.PD
.PP
Advanced, for settings most machines never need to change:
.PP
.PD 0
.TP 34
Finish leftover changes at boot
[Boot] FinishLeftover
.TP
When a device file has changed
[Load] BaselineDrift
.TP
Channel release, milliseconds
[Midi Settings] ChannelRelease
.TP
Automatic voice tuning
a submenu of the six
[Midi Settings] AutoVoice settings: Drain, Emergency, Healthy,
Settle, Floor and Tails
.PD
.PP
Settings it does not show are kept as they are in the existing file.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-f " FILE"
Write
.I FILE
instead of
.IR /etc/vlhe.conf .
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print a usage message and exit.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/vlhe.conf
The file written. The previous version is kept as
.IR /etc/vlhe.conf.old .
.TP
.I /etc/vlhe/defaults.conf
The installation defaults. Written once, the first time settings are
saved, and never again; if it exists it is left alone. The control
centre uses it if
.I /etc/vlhe.conf
is lost and for File > Reset Settings.
.TP
.IR setup-vlhe-menu ", " setup-vlhe-strings
The menu's shape and its wording, read from the directory holding
.BR setup-vlhe .
It will not start without them.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The file was written, or the user quit with
.BR q ,
or
.B \-h
was given.
.TP
.B 2
A usage error, or the menu or strings file could not be read.
.SH EXAMPLES
Set up the machine, as root:
.PP
.RS
setup-vlhe
.RE
.PP
Try the menus without touching the machine's file:
.PP
.RS
setup-vlhe \-f /tmp/test.conf
.RE
